    historyContent: {
      origins: "Zhēng Shàngyóu (争上游), meaning 'Struggling Upstream', is a Chinese climbing card game whose name is said to recall a literary image of a fish swimming against the current in early spring; some associate it with the Cultural Revolution era. The game exists in several variations and is closely related to Japanese Dai Hin Min, Vietnamese Tien Len, Chinese Big Two, and Western President. It was brought to the UK in 1979 by John McLeod, who learned it from interpreter Zhang Chuansheng during a British Go players' visit to China; in Britain it became known as Pits, referring to the predicament of the losing players. It is particularly popular in Shanghai, Zhejiang, and Jiangsu and forms the basis for many modern climbing games.",
